known: see ''acquaint'', ''v.''] '''1.''' The state of being acquainted, or of being more or less intimately conversant (used with reference to both persons and things); knowledge of; experience in: used with ''with'', and formerly sometimes with ''of''.
{{smaller block|Good Master Brook, I desire more ''acquaintance of'' you.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., ii. 2.}}
That general ''acquaintance with'' the mechanism and working of the living system which all persons, even moderately educated, should possess.
{{right|''Huxley and Youmans'', Physiol., § 368.}}
I have a very general ''acquaintance'' here in New England.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Old Manse, i.}}}}
'''2.''' A person known to one, especially a person with whom one is not on terms of great intimacy: as, he is not a friend, only an ''acquaintance''. [This is the only sense which admits of a plural form.]
{{smaller block|We see he is ashamed of his nearest ''acquaintances''.
{{right|''C. Boyle'', Bentley on Phalaris.}}
Mere ''acquaintance'' you have none; you have drawn them all into a nearer line; and they who have conversed with you, are for ever after inviolably yours.
{{right|''Dryden'', Orig. and Prog. of Satire.}}}}
'''3.''' The whole body of those with whom one is acquainted: used as a plural, as if for ''acquaintances''. See ''acquaintant''.
{{smaller block|Mine ''acquaintance'' are verily estranged from me.
{{right|Job xix. 13.}}}}
{{smaller|'''To cultivate one’s acquaintance,''' to endeavor to become intimate with one. ='''Syn. 1.''' ''Acquaintance, Familiarity, Intimacy. Acquaintance'', knowledge arising from occasional intercourse; ''familiarity'', knowledge arising from frequent or daily intercourse; ''intimacy'', unreserved intercourse, intercourse of the closest possible kind.}}

