¶
{{smaller block|Nor was his ''acquaintance'' less with the famous poets of his age, than with the noblemen and ladies. {{right|''Dryden.''}}}}
{{smaller block|The honour of Sheridan’s ''familiarity''—or supposed ''familiarity''—was better to my godfather than money. {{right|''Lamb'', My First Play.}}}}
{{smaller block|The ''intimacy'' between the father of Eugenio and Agrestis produced a tender friendship between his sister and Amelia. {{right|''Hawkesworth'', Adventurer, No. 64.}}}}

'''acquaintanceship''' (ạ-kwān′tạns-ship), ''n.'' The state of having acquaintance.

'''acquaintant†''' (ạ-kwān′tạnt), ''n.'' [< ''acquaint'' + ''-ant''<sup>1</sup>, after OF. ''acointant'', ppr. of ''acointer'', acquaint; prob. developed from ''acquaintance'', with which, in sense 3, the pl. ''acquaintants'' would nearly coincide in pronunciation.] A person with whom one is acquainted. See ''acquaintance'', 2.
{{smaller block|An ''acquaintant'' and a friend of Edmund Spenser. {{right|''I. Walton.''}}}}
{{smaller block|He and his readers are become old ''acquaintants.'' {{right|''Swift'', Tale of a Tub.}}}}

'''acquainted''' (ạ-kwān′ted), ''p. a.'' [< ''acquaint'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>. Cf. ''acquaint'', ''a.''] '''1.''' Having acquaintance; informed; having personal knowledge.
{{smaller block|''Faulk.'' What, is he much ''acquainted'' in the family?<br>''Abs.'' O, very intimate. {{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, ii. 1.}}}}
'''2†.''' Known; familiarly known; not new.
{{smaller block|Things ''acquainted'' and familiar to us. {{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., v. 2.}}}}

