'''2.''' To agree; consent; tacitly assent; quietly comply or submit: as, to ''acquiesce'' in an opinion, argument, or arrangement.
{{smaller block|Neander sent his man with a letter to Theomachus, who ''acquiesced'' to the proposal.
{{right|''Gentleman Instructed'', p. 123.}}}}
{{smaller block|Presuming on the unshaken submission of Hippolita, he flattered himself that she would . . . ''acquiesce'' with patience to a divorce.
{{right|''Walpole'', Castle of Otranto, i.}}}}
{{smaller block|Take the place and attitude which belong to you, and all men ''acquiesce''.
{{right|''Emerson'', Essays, 1st ser., p. 136.}}}}
{{smaller block|[In modern usage, ''acquiesce'' is generally followed by the preposition ''in''; formerly ''to'', ''with'', and ''from'' were in use.]}}

'''acquiescement''' (ak-wi-es′ment; F. pron. a-kyes′moṅ), ''n.'' [< F. “''acquiescement'', quietness, also an agreement” (Cotgrave): see ''acquiesce'' and ''-ment''.] In ''French'' and ''Canadian law'', acquiescence; free consent.

'''acquiescence''' (ak-wi-es′ẹns), ''n.'' [= Sp. ''aquiescencia'' = It. ''acquiescenza'', < L. as if *''acquiescentia'', < ''acquiescen(t-)s'', acquiescent: see ''acquiescent''.] '''1.''' The act of acquiescing or giving tacit assent; a silent submission, or submission with apparent consent. {{smaller|It is distinguished from avowed consent on the one hand, and from opposition or open discontent on the other: as, an ''acquiescence'' in the decisions of a court, or in the allotments of Providence.}}
{{smaller block|With the inevitable ''acquiescence'' of all public servants, [he] resumes his composure and goes on.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Snow Image.}}}}
{{smaller block|There is a certain grave ''acquiescence'' in ignorance, a recognition of our impotence to solve momentous and urgent questions, which has a satisfaction of its own.
{{right|''J. H. Newman'', Gram. of Assent, p. 198.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''acquirability''' (a-kwīr-ạ-bil′ị-ti), ''n.'' The quality of being acquirable. ''Paley.'' [Rare.]

'''acquirable''' (a-kwīr′ạ-bl), ''a.'' [< ''acquire'' + ''-able.'' Cf. Sp. ''adquirible,'' Pg. ''adquirivel.''] Capable of being acquired.

'''acquire''' (a-kwīr′), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''acquired,'' ppr. ''acquiring.'' [< ME. ''aqwere'' (rare), < OF. ''acquerre,'' later ''aquerir,'' F. ''acquérir,'' acquire, get, = Sp. Pg. ''adquirir,'' < L. ''acquærere,'' a collateral form of ''acquirere,'' acquire, get, obtain, < ''ad,'' to, + ''quærere,'' seek: see ''query.'' The E. word is now spelled with ''i'' instead of ''e,'' to bring it nearer to the Latin. Cf. ''inquire, require.''] To get or gain, the object being something which is more or less permanent, or which becomes vested or inherent in the subject: as, to ''acquire'' a title, estate, learning, habits, skill, dominion, etc.; to ''acquire'' a stammer; sugar ''acquires'' a brown color by being burned. {{smaller|A mere temporary possession is not expressed by ''acquire,'' but by ''obtain, procure,'' etc.: as, to ''obtain'' (not ''acquire'') a book on loan.}}
{{smaller block|Descent is the title whereby a man, on the death of his ancestor, ''acquires'' his estate by right of representation, as his heir at law.
{{right|''Blackstone.''}}}}
{{smaller block|Having been left in a greater degree than others to manage their own affairs, the English people have become self-helping, and have ''acquired'' great practical ability.
{{right|''H. Spencer,'' Social Statics, p. 429.}}}}
{{smaller block|Men ''acquire'' faculties by practice.
{{right|''W. K. Clifford,'' Lectures, I. 94.}}}}
{{smaller block|The young demand thoughts that find an echo in their real and not their ''acquired'' nature, and care very little about the dress they are put in.
{{right|''Lowell,'' Study Windows, p. 406.}}}}
'''Acquired logic.''' {{smaller|See ''logic.''}} = '''Syn.''' {{smaller|To get, obtain, gain, attain, procure, win, earn, secure, gather, master, learn. See ''attain.''}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
'''acquirement''' (a-kwīr′ment), ''n.'' '''1.''' The act of acquiring; especially, the gaining of knowledge or mental attributes.
{{smaller block|It is very difficult to lay down rules for the ''acquirement'' of such a taste.
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 409.}}}}
'''2.''' That which is acquired; attainment: commonly in the plural.
{{smaller block|His ''acquirements'' by industry were enriched and enlarged by many excellent endowments of nature.
{{right|''Sir J. Hayward'', Raigne of Edward VI.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' Gathering, gaining.—'''2.''' ''Acquirements, Acquisitions, Attainments, Accomplishments, Endowments, Enduements''; gain, resources. ''Acquisitions'' is the most general term, but it is gradually being restricted to material gains. ''Attainments'' denotes exclusively intellectual or moral acquisitions: as, a man of great ''attainments''; his spiritual ''attainments'' were high. ''Acquirements'' has nearly the same meaning as ''attainments'', though it is sometimes loosely used as equivalent to ''acquisitions''; it has more direct reference to particular things acquired: as, skill in boxing was among his ''acquirements''. ''Accomplishments'' are attainments or acquirements, particularly such as fit the possessor for society: as, French, dancing, and music were the sum of her ''accomplishments''. ''Endowments'' are the gifts of nature, as genius or aptitude. ''Enduements'' are endowments, acquirements, or attainments in the field of moral and spiritual life, but they are opposed to ''attainments'' in being regarded as gifts from heaven rather than as the result of personal endeavor. See ''endue''<sup>2</sup>.}}
{{smaller block|When you are disposed to be vain of your mental ''acquirements'', look up to those who are more accomplished than yourself.
{{right|''Dr. J. Moore.''}}
Interference has been sanctioned, . . . either in the purely domestic concerns of a nation, or with respect to its foreign relations and territorial ''acquisitions''.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XIII. 192.}}
It is in general more profitable to reckon up our defects than to boast of our ''attainments''.
{{right|''Carlyle'', Essays.}}}}

readings.c3s4:
{{smaller block|<poem>I danced the polka and cellarius,
Spun glass, stuffed birds, and modeled flowers in wax,
Because she liked ''accomplishments'' in girls.</poem>
{{right|''Mrs. Browning'', Aurora Leigh, i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|He ought to think no man valuable but for his public spirit, justice, and integrity; and all other ''endowments'' to be esteemed only as they contribute to the exerting those virtues. {{right|''Steele'', Spectator, No. 340.}}}}

'''acquirer''' (a̤-kwīr′ėr), ''n.'' One who acquires.

'''acquiry†''' (a̤-kwīr′i), ''n.'' [< ''acquire'' + ''-y'', after ''inquiry''.] Acquirement.
{{smaller block|No art requireth more hard study and pain toward the ''acquiry'' of it than contentment. {{right|''Barrow'', Sermons, III. 62.}}}}
